                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "25554d7b-d8f6-3d38-b9b4-787c3aa11de8",
                                        "entry_id": "b192afe3-676d-397d-a677-65c0c7100256",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roughly dome-shaped piece of armor, especially one covering the shoulder, the elbow, or the knee."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "565bea39-00d9-49c4-9a05-cf7c291e0416",
                                                "sense_id": "25554d7b-d8f6-3d38-b9b4-787c3aa11de8",
                                                "text": "[…] the elbow cop or coudiere for the elbow; and the rerebrace or arriere-bras for the upper arm. The shoulder cop, pauldron or epauliere which covered the shoulder, and often a large part of the breast and back, was usually considered a part of the arm guard."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9abf6b64-c3f8-4d2c-8e68-27495575d30f",
                                                "sense_id": "25554d7b-d8f6-3d38-b9b4-787c3aa11de8",
                                                "text": "In the middle was a pile of armour – breastplates, helmets, vambraces, gorgets, pauldrons, cops, cuisses, sabatons, gauntlets, all mangled and ruined, ..."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4322eb11-d52e-4c8b-8061-11065eb5341d",
                                                "sense_id": "25554d7b-d8f6-3d38-b9b4-787c3aa11de8",
                                                "text": "Tilting Cuisses 457. In the 15th century the knee cops were merged in the plate cuisses. In the East, except in Japan, knee cops as separate pieces of armor were seldom used east of Turkey."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
